    'speak.help.html': '<p><b>What the page does.</b> The sentence is split into words and each word gets its transcription in the <b>International Phonetic Alphabet</b> (IPA) from Wiktionary. A word Wiktionary has only in its dictionary form (Häuser → Haus) shows that form\'s IPA marked ≈ and is not scored; a word without any transcription is grey and lets anything through.</p><p><b>Listening.</b> 🔊 says the sentence or a word with the voice of your browser (or a downloaded one). A tap on a single sound plays a sample recording of that sound from Wikimedia Commons, usually the sound alone and between vowels (“a, apa”).</p><p><b>Recording.</b> ● records you. A <b>phone recogniser</b> (an open model, run in your browser) writes down the sounds it heard, without knowing the language or the sentence: it hears sounds, not words. The two transcriptions are then lined up sound by sound.</p><p><b>Reading the result.</b> Green is the same sound, amber a close one, red a different one, — a sound missing or added. Treat it as a hint, not a grade: the recogniser makes mistakes too, Wiktionary often writes the dictionary pronunciation rather than every detail, and accents differ.</p><p><b>Asking an AI.</b> Copy the prompt below and paste it into an assistant: <b>Claude</b> (claude.ai, the Claude app, or <b>Claude in Chrome</b>, which can also read this page), <b>ChatGPT</b>, <b>Google Gemini</b>, <b>Microsoft Copilot</b> or <b>Mistral Le Chat</b>. Assistants that accept audio (ChatGPT, Gemini, Claude in some apps) can also be given your recording: download it with ⬇.</p>',
    'speak.howto.intro': '<p>The <b>IPA</b> gives every sound of every language one symbol, so [ʃ] is the same sound in German <i>Schule</i>, French <i>chat</i> and Portuguese <i>chá</i>. Slashes /…/ write a language\'s sounds broadly; square brackets […] write them narrowly. ˈ marks the stressed syllable, ː a long sound.</p><p>The <b>consonant chart</b> is a grid: the <b>columns</b> say <b>where</b> in the mouth the air is blocked, from the lips (left) to the throat (right); the <b>rows</b> say <b>how</b> it is blocked. In a cell, the left symbol is <b>voiceless</b> and the right one <b>voiced</b> (the vocal folds buzz — put a finger on your throat and say <i>ssss</i>, then <i>zzzz</i>).</p>',
